---
import PaperLayout from '../../layouts/PaperLayout.astro';

const authors = [
  { name: 'Baekseung Kim', affiliation: 'SNU' },
  { name: 'Junhoo Lee', affiliation: 'KAIST' },
  { name: 'Sohyeon Hong', affiliation: 'SNU' },
  { name: 'Suhyun Jeon', affiliation: 'SNU' },
  { name: 'Minkyu Kim', affiliation: 'SNU' },
  { name: 'Seungyeon Kim', affiliation: 'SNU' },
  { name: 'Donghoon Han', href: 'https://dhk1349.github.io/', affiliation: 'Dnotitia', me: true },
  { name: 'Nojun Kwak', href: 'http://mipal.snu.ac.kr/index.php/Nojun_Kwak', affiliation: 'SNU' },
];

const links = [
  { label: 'arXiv', href: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19957', meta: '2610.19957' },
];
---

<PaperLayout
  title="Complementary Future Token Pruning"
  subtitle="for World Action Models"
  venue="Preprint · 2026"
  authors={authors}
  links={links}
  description="CF-Prune is a training-free method that prunes future-frame tokens in world action models, using instruction and action attention from the first denoising pass to keep only what action generation needs."
>
  <section>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/cf-prune/img/overview.png"
        alt="CF-Prune overview: instruction and action attention from the first denoising step build a pruning mask over future-video tokens, and phase identification switches pruning off during precision phases"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Overview of CF-Prune. In the first denoising step (<em>k</em> = 1), instruction and
        action attention select which future-video tokens to keep; phase identification
        decides whether to prune for the remaining steps (<em>k</em> = 2, …, <em>K</em>).
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Abstract</h2>
    <p>
      World action models (WAMs) jointly predict future visual states and robot actions,
      but processing dense future-frame tokens incurs substantial inference latency. We
      propose <strong>CF-Prune</strong>, a training-free framework for pruning
      future-frame tokens in pretrained WAMs. Our key insight is that future visual
      predictions support action generation and therefore need not preserve every visual
      detail required for high-fidelity video synthesis. CF-Prune combines instruction and
      action attention to identify task-relevant tokens during the initial denoising
      forward pass, even when future-frame and action representations remain highly noisy.
      An entropy-based phase identification mechanism further retains dense computation
      during precision phases to mitigate pruning-induced performance degradation.
      Experiments on RoboLab, RoboCasa, and real-world manipulation tasks demonstrate
      consistent inference speedups. At a 35% pruning ratio, CF-Prune achieves average
      speedups of 1.19–1.27× while improving task success rates. At a 50% pruning ratio, it
      achieves 1.29–1.33× average speedups on both simulation benchmarks, with overall
      success rates within 1.0 percentage point of the baseline.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Key numbers</h2>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="stat-num">0</div>
        <div class="stat-label">Extra training steps</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="stat-num">1.19–1.27×</div>
        <div class="stat-label">Average speedup at 35% pruning</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="stat-num">85.3 → 90.7%</div>
        <div class="stat-label">Real-world success at 35%</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <div class="stat-num">1.33×</div>
        <div class="stat-label">RoboLab speedup at 50%</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>1. The problem: future frames dominate the compute</h2>
    <div class="split">
      <figure class="narrow">
        <img
          src="/cf-prune/img/motivation.png"
          alt="Dense WAM keeps every future-video token at full token cost, while future-token pruning keeps only task-relevant tokens at reduced cost and still predicts the actions"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Rethinking future-video generation for action prediction.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div>
        <p>
          World action models build on video diffusion backbones and denoise future video
          and robot actions together. After VAE compression, a future video is still a
          spatiotemporal grid of <em>T<sub>v</sub></em> × <em>H<sub>v</sub></em> ×
          <em>W<sub>v</sub></em> tokens, far outnumbering the <em>H<sub>a</sub></em> action
          tokens and dominating every denoising step.
        </p>
        <p>
          But a WAM uses future frames as an intermediate reference for action generation,
          not as a video to show anyone. They must keep task-relevant semantics and
          dynamics, not every visual detail — so a dense grid is redundant for action
          prediction.
        </p>
        <p>
          Pruning these tokens is not straightforward: they are the model's outputs,
          denoised from Gaussian noise, so their relevance is hard to judge early, and
          judging it late forfeits the speedup. Existing WAM acceleration reduces denoising
          steps or redesigns the model, which requires retraining.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>2. Method</h2>
    <p>
      CF-Prune makes its token selection once, inside the initial denoising forward pass,
      and applies it for the rest of denoising.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Instruction-based spatial prior.</strong> Observation tokens carry no
        diffusion noise, so observation-to-instruction attention marks instruction-relevant
        regions of the scene. The scoring layer is the one whose attention is most
        concentrated (lowest normalized entropy).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Action-based visual relevance.</strong> Action-to-future attention measures
        which regions the action tokens rely on — per frame for the moving wrist camera,
        shared across frames for fixed third-person views. A 5 × 5 Gaussian blur keeps the
        selection spatially contiguous.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Complementary selection.</strong> For pruning ratio α, keep the top
        (1 − α)/2 tokens by action relevance, then another (1 − α)/2 by instruction
        relevance among the rest.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Phase identification.</strong> An entropy contrast across camera views and
        early vs. late actions detects precision phases near object interaction; pruning is
        switched off there and applied only in coarse phases.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>3. Results</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="results">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>RoboLab</th>
            <th>Simple</th>
            <th>Moderate</th>
            <th>Complex</th>
            <th>Overall</th>
            <th>Speedup</th>
            <th>FLOPs</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="method">Dense</td><td>40.3</td><td>34.1</td><td>27.6</td><td>36.5</td><td>1.00×</td><td>100%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="method">SpecPrune-VLA (obs + future)</td><td>36.9</td><td>28.2</td><td>23.5</td><td>32.2</td><td>1.23×</td><td>77.5%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="method">SP-VLA (obs + future)</td><td>37.5</td><td>32.3</td><td>23.5</td><td>33.8</td><td>1.24×</td><td>77.5%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="method">Sparse VideoGen</td><td>21.9</td><td>16.9</td><td>7.1</td><td>18.2</td><td>0.97×</td><td>104.7%</td></tr>
          <tr class="highlight"><td class="method">CF-Prune, α = 35%</td><td>43.0</td><td>33.6</td><td>28.2</td><td>37.8</td><td>1.23×</td><td>73.2%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="method">CF-Prune, α = 50%</td><td>40.3</td><td>32.8</td><td>24.7</td><td>35.7</td><td>1.33×</td><td>61.7%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="table-caption">
      Success (%) on 120 RoboLab tasks with Cosmos3-Nano-Policy-DROID; speedup and FLOPs
      are averaged over all inference calls. Latency on an RTX A6000. The paper also
      reports observation-only variants of the VLA pruning baselines.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>RoboLab.</strong> At α = 35%, CF-Prune is 1.23× faster with 26.8% fewer FLOPs
      and raises overall success from 36.5% to 37.8%. At α = 50% it reaches 1.33× with only
      a 0.8 pp drop. Extending VLA pruning masks to future frames costs more success at
      similar speed, and Sparse VideoGen's profiling overhead outweighs its savings.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>RoboCasa.</strong> At α = 35%, CF-Prune is 1.19× faster with 15.6% fewer FLOPs
      and lifts overall success from 65.0% to 69.0%. At α = 50% (1.29×) overall success is
      64.0%, but navigation drops from 38.0% to 18.0%, so the effect of aggressive pruning
      varies by task.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/cf-prune/img/real_world_setup.png"
        alt="Real-world setup with a Franka arm and three cameras, and the button-pressing, pick-and-place and block-stacking tasks"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Real-world setup and the three tasks: e-button pressing, block pick-and-place, and
        block stacking.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="results">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Real world</th>
            <th>Button</th>
            <th>Pick-and-place</th>
            <th>Stacking</th>
            <th>Overall</th>
            <th>Speedup</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="method">Dense</td><td>84.0</td><td>92.0</td><td>80.0</td><td>85.3</td><td>1.00×</td></tr>
          <tr class="highlight"><td class="method">CF-Prune, α = 35%</td><td>88.0</td><td>96.0</td><td>88.0</td><td>90.7</td><td>1.27×</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="table-caption">
      Success (%) over 25 rollouts per task on a Franka Emika Panda with wrist, right and
      front cameras.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/cf-prune/img/pruning_examples.png"
        alt="Real-world future-token pruning masks at 35% and 50%; navy cells mark pruned positions, concentrated away from the gripper and target objects"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Future-token pruning at 35% and 50% on recorded real-world inputs. Navy cells
        indicate pruned positions.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>4. Ablations</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Both signals are needed.</strong> At 35% pruning without phase
      identification, instruction-only selection reaches 34.0% on RoboLab and action-only
      30.8%; the complementary action-first selection reaches 35.3%, above instruction-first
      ordering (34.3%).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Phase identification helps.</strong> Keeping dense computation in detected
      precision phases lifts overall success from 35.3% to 37.8% while flagging only 31.4%
      of inference calls, so the average speedup stays at 1.23×.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Takeaway</h2>
    <p>
      Treating future frames as support for action prediction, not as video to be rendered
      faithfully, exposes redundancy that a pretrained WAM can shed at inference time. The
      attention available in the very first, still-noisy denoising pass is enough to decide
      which future tokens action generation needs.
    </p>
  </section>

